userStories.description:
**Goal** — Add pure conversion functions that represent one ACP turn as a native user/assistant message pair with ACP origin and bounded tool-name summaries.

**Motivation** — Resuming a chat session that ran on an agent replays only the native document (`ChatSessionHandle.history`), so an agent's earlier turns are missing from the resumed TUI transcript.

**Approach** — `acpTurnMessages` builds the assistant `content` as: the reply text — the `turn_end` event's `output` when it is non-empty, else the `text_delta` texts concatenated in order (a `stream_reset` discards the text collected so far) — then one line per `tool_call` in call order, `[tool] <name>`, or `[tool] <name> (error)` when the `tool_result` with the same `callId` has `isError: true`, with the name cut to `ACP_TOOL_NAME_MAX_CHARS` code points. At most `ACP_TOOL_SUMMARY_LIMIT` lines are written, then `[tool] … and <n> more`. Text and the first line are separated by one newline (no leading newline when there is no text). With neither text nor tool calls the content is `(no reply; turn <status>)` from the `turn_end` event. `thinking_delta` text and tool inputs and previews are never recorded. The assistant message has `origin` and no `toolCalls` or `thinking`.

`origin` is nax-ai's existing "who wrote this message" field (`ConversationMessage` in `packages/nax-ai/src/protocols/types.ts`): pi-ai treats a message from another provider as another model's, and the recorded message has no thinking to drop. Tool calls are summaries, not structured `toolCalls` / `tool-result` messages, because the native model has no such tools to pair them with.

**Scope** — In: create `packages/nax/src/chat/acp/acp-history.ts` and its unit tests; derive ChatMessage from TranscriptDoc via @nathapp/nax-agent, not a forbidden nax-ai import. Out: unseen-message selection/entries (US-002), bounds/prompt (US-003), store writes (US-004), wrapping (US-005/006) and opener/barrel/TUI wiring (US-007). Recording an ACP turn's tool calls as structured native `toolCalls` / `tool-result` messages, or carrying tool inputs, results or thinking; a turn's tools are recorded as one `[tool] <name>` line each.

**Interface** — verbatim TypeScript:
```ts
export type ChatMessage = TranscriptDoc["messages"][number];
export const ACP_TOOL_SUMMARY_LIMIT = 20;
export const ACP_TOOL_NAME_MAX_CHARS = 80;
/** `{ provider: "acp:<agent>", model: acp.model ?? "default" }`; the effort is not part of it. */
export function acpOrigin(acp: ChatAcpModel): { readonly provider: string; readonly model: string };
/** The ACP chat agent that wrote an assistant message, from `origin.provider`; undefined otherwise. */
export function acpAgentOfMessage(message: ChatMessage): AcpChatAgent | undefined;
/** The user message and the assistant message that record one ACP turn. */
export function acpTurnMessages(input: {
  readonly message: string;
  readonly events: readonly SessionEvent[];
  readonly acp: ChatAcpModel;
}): readonly [ChatMessage, ChatMessage];
```

**Goal** — Compute an opening agent's unseen native-history suffix and convert it into capped speaker/text handoff entries.

**Motivation** — User ruling 2026-10-29 (design §6.4): history carries over on a switch, in every direction (native to ACP, ACP to native, ACP to another ACP agent), the same experience as switching native models today.

**Approach** — Hand an opening agent the turns it has not seen. When the ACP branch opens an agent:
- a fresh agent session (no agent document, or the F2 fallback after an unusable one) has seen none of the native document;
- a resumed agent session has seen everything up to and including the last assistant message whose `origin.provider` is `acp:<that agent>`; it has not seen what follows. With no such message (a session from before this feature), it has seen none.

One entry per user message and per assistant message (tool-result messages are dropped; an assistant message with neither text nor tool calls is dropped; `thinking` is ignored). Lengths are JavaScript string lengths (UTF-16 code units). An entry's text longer than `ACP_HANDOFF_ENTRY_MAX_CHARS` (4,000) is cut to its first 4,000 units followed by `…`.

Speakers: `User`; `nax` for an assistant message with no `origin`; `nax (<origin.model>)` for an `origin` whose provider does not start with `acp:`; the agent label (`Claude Code`, `Codex`, `Cursor`) for `acp:<agent>`; the raw provider for an `acp:` provider naming another agent.

`handoffEntries` maps a user message to speaker `User`; an assistant message to the speaker of "Handoff text" above, with text = its `content`, then one `[tool] <name>` line per `toolCalls` entry (one newline between, no leading newline when `content` is empty).

**Scope** — In: new `packages/nax/src/chat/acp/acp-handoff-entries.ts`, reusing US-001 ChatMessage/origin recognition and existing agent labels. Out: overall 40,000-character suffix bound, notices and prompt rendering (US-003), wrapper/delivery/recording (US-005/006), opener freshness decisions and wiring (US-007). A dedicated speaker for a native compaction summary (a user message starting with `COMPACTION_SUMMARY_PREFIX`); it is handed over as a `User:` entry. Keeping UTF-16 surrogate pairs whole when an entry is cut at 4,000 units. Native compaction that summarises away every message an agent wrote: that agent's next reopen is then handed the whole native history again, including turns it already saw. An agent session opened before this feature has no recorded turns of its own, so its first reopen is handed the whole native history, possibly repeating what it saw.

**Interface** — verbatim TypeScript:
```ts
export const ACP_HANDOFF_ENTRY_MAX_CHARS = 4_000;
export interface HandoffEntry { readonly speaker: string; readonly text: string }
/** The messages an opening agent has not seen (see Approach 3). */
export function unseenMessages(
  messages: readonly ChatMessage[],
  agent: AcpChatAgent,
  fresh: boolean,
): readonly ChatMessage[];
/** One entry per user / assistant message (Approach 4), each text cut to the entry cap; no total bound. */
export function handoffEntries(messages: readonly ChatMessage[]): readonly HandoffEntry[];
```

**Goal** — Introduce an AgentSession-preserving wrapper that synchronously forwards sends and prefixes a handoff until a terminal turn shows agent work.

**Motivation** — User ruling 2026-10-29 (design §6.4): history carries over on a switch, in every direction (native to ACP, ACP to native, ACP to another ACP agent), the same experience as switching native models today.

**Approach** — `recordAcpTurns` returns an `AgentSession` that reads `id`, `backend` and `lastTurn` from the wrapped session when they are read (getters, so a stub without them still wraps), forwards `answer`, `cancel`, `close` and (when present) `runCommand`, and implements `send(message)` by calling the wrapped session's `send` synchronously with `handoff + "\n\n" + message` while a handoff is pending (else `message`), forwarding every event unchanged and in order.

The handoff is cleared once a send reaches `turn_end` having shown agent work. This is a deliberately narrower evidence set than the one the ACP backend uses to mark its instructions delivered (#2364: `receipt()` in `events.ts` also fires on usage, plan and tool-update updates and on settle); a turn the backend counts as delivered but that showed no text, thinking or tool call keeps the handoff pending and is not recorded (accepted, Out of Scope). A turn with no agent work (an auth or quota failure before the agent saw the prompt) is not recorded either, so the agent's last-seen point never moves past turns it was not given.

Agent work means a non-empty `text_delta`, a non-empty `thinking_delta` or a `tool_call`. The wrapped `send` throws, or its iteration ends without `turn_end`: The error (if any) propagates; nothing is recorded; a pending handoff stays pending for the next send. With no handoff, the first message is sent as typed. ACP sends the session's system instructions only on the first prompt of a new session (`instructionsSent` in `backend.ts`), so a fresh agent receives instructions, then the handoff, then the message; a resumed one receives the handoff and the message.

**Scope** — In: create `packages/nax/src/chat/acp/acp-recorder.ts` with forwarding, pending-handoff state and worked-terminal detection; create forwarding/handoff tests. Preserve receiver binding and synchronous busy errors. Out: actual conversion/appending and `_acpRecorderDeps = { appendNativeTurn }` (US-006), which extends this file after US-004 exists; history selection/rendering (US-002/003); opener/store wiring (US-007). This story has no dependencies: its final interface accepts store/sessionId/acp but must not import modules only created by other independent stories. Recording, or clearing the handoff for, a turn the ACP backend counts as delivered (usage or plan updates only) but that showed no text, thinking or tool call; such a turn keeps the handoff pending and is not recorded. Showing the handoff text in the TUI transcript.

**Interface** — verbatim TypeScript (recording seam added by US-006):
```ts
export interface RecordAcpTurnsInput {
  /** The native chat transcript store (the one at the sessions dir), not the agent's. */
  readonly store: TranscriptStore;
  readonly sessionId: string;
  readonly acp: ChatAcpModel;
  /** Rendered handoff, put in front of the first message sent; empty or absent means none. */
  readonly handoff?: string;
}
export function recordAcpTurns(session: AgentSession, input: RecordAcpTurnsInput): AgentSession;
```

**Goal** — Extend the US-005 wrapper to append each terminal worked turn before iteration completion while retaining pending handoff on unworked or incomplete turns.

**Motivation** — The ACP backend stores only its ACP record and cost baseline there, never the messages (`packages/nax-agent-acp/src/client/backend.ts` `saveBaseline` / `saveFinal`), so nobody owns an ACP brain's message history yet.

**Approach** — Record every ACP turn in the native document. `openChatSession` wraps an ACP brain's session (`recordAcpTurns`). After each turn that reaches `turn_end` having shown agent work (a non-empty `text_delta`, a non-empty `thinking_delta` or a `tool_call`), the wrapper appends two messages to the native chat transcript document (the store at `request.storage.dir`, the one the native brain resumes from): the person's message as typed, and one assistant message holding the agent's reply text (`turn_end.output`, or the concatenated `text_delta` texts when it is empty), a one-line `[tool] <name>` summary per tool call, and `origin: { provider: "acp:<agent>", model: <model or "default"> }`.

When the wrapped iteration ends with a `turn_end` and showed agent work, it clears the handoff and awaits `_acpRecorderDeps.appendNativeTurn(store, sessionId, acpTurnMessages({ message, events, acp }))` before its own iteration completes, so `runChatTurn` (which consumes the whole iteration before writing session metadata) sees the turn recorded.

Failure handling:
- The native store's `load` or `save` rejects while recording a turn: `appendNativeTurn` resolves; `getLogger().warn("chat", "could not record the ACP turn", { sessionId, error })`; the send completes normally with every event delivered; the turn is missing from the native document.
- The wrapped `send` throws, or its iteration ends without `turn_end`: The error (if any) propagates; nothing is recorded; a pending handoff stays pending for the next send.
- A turn reaches `turn_end` with no agent work (no non-empty `text_delta`, no non-empty `thinking_delta`, no `tool_call`): Not recorded; a pending handoff stays pending.
- A turn with agent work ends `cancelled`, `timed_out`, `interrupted` or `errored`: It is recorded like a completed turn (the reply so far, or `(no reply; turn <status>)` for thinking only).

This is a deliberately narrower evidence set than the one the ACP backend uses to mark its instructions delivered (#2364: `receipt()` in `events.ts` also fires on usage, plan and tool-update updates and on settle); a turn the backend counts as delivered but that showed no text, thinking or tool call keeps the handoff pending and is not recorded (accepted, Out of Scope).

**Scope** — In: extend `packages/nax/src/chat/acp/acp-recorder.ts` with US-001 acpTurnMessages, US-004 appendNativeTurn and the exported test seam; create `acp-recorder-record.test.ts` and preserve US-005 forwarding behavior. The original typed message, never the handoff-prefixed input, is recorded. Out: changes to message conversion/store append implementations (US-001/004), prompt building (US-003) and opener/barrel/TUI integration (US-007). Recording a turn that throws or ends without `turn_end`. Retrying, or showing in the TUI, a failed recording; it is logged as a warning only. Recording, or clearing the handoff for, a turn the ACP backend counts as delivered (usage or plan updates only) but that showed no text, thinking or tool call; such a turn keeps the handoff pending and is not recorded.

**Interface** — verbatim TypeScript (extend the existing US-005 file):
```ts
export interface RecordAcpTurnsInput {
  /** The native chat transcript store (the one at the sessions dir), not the agent's. */
  readonly store: TranscriptStore;
  readonly sessionId: string;
  readonly acp: ChatAcpModel;
  /** Rendered handoff, put in front of the first message sent; empty or absent means none. */
  readonly handoff?: string;
}
/** Test seam. */
export const _acpRecorderDeps: { appendNativeTurn: typeof appendNativeTurn };
export function recordAcpTurns(session: AgentSession, input: RecordAcpTurnsInput): AgentSession;
```

Seam verification: The seam AC stubs `_acpRecorderDeps.appendNativeTurn` and asserts its arguments. Use a deferred append promise for the AC that gates iteration completion, not timing sleeps.
